    createExhibit(5, '5. Battles of the Center, Deir Yassin & Ramla',
      `<strong>5.1 Ramot Menashe</strong><br>Sindiani, Barki, Sabarin, Khubayza, Umm-Shuf - The villages in the Ephraim Hills. Irgun combat units conquered five Arab villages on the border of the "Triangle" in the Ephraim Hills: Sindiani, Barki, Sabarin, Khubeiza and Um-Shouf. The battle was fought by "Etzel" infantry forces supported by 3" mortars. Twenty of the local fighters were killed and 120 were taken prisoners. In addition, a large quantity of weapons and ammunition was captured. None of the "Etzel" fighters were hurt.<br><br><strong>5.2 Rosh HaAyin</strong><button class="audio-guide-btn inline-audio-btn" aria-pressed="false" onclick="playAudioTrack('audio/en-5-2.mp3', this)" aria-label="Play recording: Capture of Rosh Ha’Ayin">🎧</button><details class="transcript-toggle"><summary>Show transcript</summary><div class="transcript-text">This area displays the capture of Arab villages by the Etzel during the War of Independence. One of these villages was Ras el Ein – known today as Rosh Ha'ayin.
According to the Partition Plan, Rosh Ha'ayin was slated to be part of the Arab state. The springs of the Yarkon River, located in this area, served as Jerusalem's most important source of water and there was genuine concern that the Arab forces under the command of Hassan Salameh would contaminate or block this precious water supply.
Therefore, at the end of May 1948, the Etzel and the Haganah decided to capture Rosh Ha'ayin and take control of the Yarkon river springs from the Arabs. After Hassan Salameh was killed in the battle and his men retreated, the Etzel forces captured the area.
A few days later, Salameh's men regrouped and attacked Rosh Ha'ayin. Too few to hold the area, the Etzel members had to retreat. Subsequently Rosh Ha'ayin and the Yarkon river springs were re-captured by the IDF's Alexandroni Brigade.</div></details><br>At the end of May, 1948, "Etzel" forces set out to occupy the sources of the Yarkon River at Rosh Ha'Ayin and the pumping station supplying water to Jerusalem. The operation was planned in cooperation with the "Haganah", in the context of Plan "D". The objective was to occupy Rosh Ha'Ayin while the "Haganah" forces attacked Tira in the "Little Triangle", and thus to prevent reinforcements reaching the enemy positions under attack. The operation HQ was set up in the vicinity of the Kfar Sirkin airfield. The plan was to attack from two directions and was set for 22nd Iyar (May 30, 1948). At dawn the force attacked Antipater's Fortress and the water pumping facilities there, and succeeded in occupying it. The Iraqi soldiers fled. Later, the second force attacked and occupied the abandoned British military camp and the railway station. However, the force did not dig in properly at the end of the battle and was not prepared to repel an enemy counterattack, because under the agreement with the "Haganah" infantry troops were supposed to arrive that same morning to take command of the area from "Etzel". That very day the Arab's counter-attacked with 300 men in armored cars. They were led by Hassan Salameh, Commander of the Arab Sector of the Central Region of Palestine, who was fatally wounded in the exchange of fire and afterwards died of his wounds. The "Etzel" casualties in the Rosh Ha'Ayin operation totalled 12, most of them killed in the counter-attack. After this attack the place again fell into Arab hands, and remained so until its liberation on July 31, 1948.<br><br><strong>5.3 The Battle for Yehud (Yahudiya)</strong><button class="audio-guide-btn inline-audio-btn" aria-pressed="false" onclick="playAudioTrack('audio/en-5-3.mp3', this)" aria-label="Play recording: The Story of Ruth">🎧</button><details class="transcript-toggle"><summary>Show transcript</summary><div class="transcript-text">We're now standing in a special part of the museum which recounts the story of two Etzel heroines.
On May 4th, 1948, ten days before the Declaration of Independence, the village of Yehudiyya (modern day Yehud) was captured by Etzel forces.  The village was the local center of operations for gangs from the Lod district, headed by Hassan Salameh. Most of the Arab assailants who attacked Jewish convoys in this area were based in Yehudiyya.
Five weeks later, on June 11th, 1948, a large, well-armed unit belonging to the Arab Legion made a surprise attack against Etzel's forces in Yehudiyya, and the village fell.
As the retreating Etzel forces were about to depart, a voice came through on their receiver:  "Commander, I can hear Arab voices and I don't know what to do." It was the voice of Miriam Aharoni- the watcher who had remained on the bulwark of the local mosque and was now surrounded by Arab forces.
Commander Ruth Moritz, a new immigrant from Australia, grabbed a sub-machine gun and ran towards the mosque to rescue her subordinate.
Ruth managed to reach the mosque, climbed up the spiral staircase and found Miriam, frightened and helpless. The two immediately decided to fight to the last bullet.
When their ammunition ran out, preferring death to being taken captive by the Arabs, the women jumped from the bulwark.
Ruth, who was known by her code name "Devorah," had been engaged to Etzel fighter Avraham Canaani who was killed two weeks earlier in the battle of Jaffa. Instead of staying home to mourn him, she had returned to the front, where she fell heroically.</div></details><br>At dawn on May 4, 1948, an "Etzel" force attacked the large Arab village, Yahudiya, which was defended by about 300 Arab fighters equipped with many weapons of various kinds. The force attacked from three sides, and after strong opposition the village fell into its hands. During the battle all the villagers fled, and when the attackers entered the place they found it empty of people. One "Etzel" soldier fell during this battle. After a few days the force was replaced by the "Etzel"'s 11th company of the Tel Aviv Region, which settled in to defend the village. On May 19, 1948, the company attacked Wilhelma aiming to draw Arab forces in that direction, in order to alleviate the situation of the "Etzel" troops attacking Ramla at that time. The intention was to create a diversion, and if the possibility arose - to take Wilhelma. The company came right up to the fences of Wilhelma but encountered fierce opposition and was forced to withdraw to Yahudiya. Its losses in that battle were 3 dead and a number of wounded. Towards the end of May the company was transferred to Ramla and was replaced by another force, which remained to defend Yahudiya. The new force included a reduced company, about 90 men, among whom were service personnel and some 20 women who served also on guard and look-out duty. The defenders had only a very little ammunition. The company commander appealed for help, and mainly for ammunition, to the I.D.F. authorities in the region, but none was forthcoming. On June 11, 1948, a large unit of the Arab Legion attacked Yahudiya, composed of irregular troops under armored cover. The defenders' ammunition ran out very quickly and contact was lost between headquarters and the outposts. Despite this, the majority of the soldiers stationed in the positions succeeded in finding their way to the center of the village. They retreated in the direction of Petach Tikva and Tel Hashomer and regrouped. But as a result of the speedy breakthrough of the enemy troops, the south-western position was cut off. All attempts by the commander to rescue the soldiers manning the post were foiled and all five of them fell in the battle. Simultaneously, the enemy took over the village mosque. On top of the mosque tower two women soldiers were stationed on look-out duty - they were both killed. During the retreat and the rescue attempts 5 more "Etzel" fighters fell. 12 soldiers died on that day, among them the two women. In all of the Yahudiya and Wilhelma battles 16 "Etzel" soldiers were killed. On July 10, 1948 Yahudiya was finally liberated by the I.D.F. forces in the "Danny" campaign. Today it has become a flourishing Jewish settlement, called Yehud.<br><br><strong>5.4 Deir Yassin</strong><br>The operational plan for the occupation of Deir Yassin determined that a force of a company would participate, consisting of two platoons of Irgun men and one platoon of "Lehi" men. On 29 Iyar (April 9, 1948) at 02:00 the boats set out for the missions. During the briefing before departure, it was decided to give up the element of surprise provided that the safety of civilians was ensured by operating loudspeakers calling on women, children and the elderly to evacuate the place. The landing of the battle was not as planned. The Arabs noticed the movement of the attackers before zero hour and opened fire, while the armored car with the loudspeaker did not reach the outskirts of the village because the "Lehi" unit in the car got stuck on an Arab roadblock half a kilometer from the village. The armored car got stuck and the calls to the residents of Deir Yassin were swallowed up in the shooting. From this moment on, the fighters were forced to advance amidst growing resistance from the Arabs. Intelligence was not comprehensive and accurate and the assessment regarding the resistance force was mistaken. Almost every house in the village served as a resistance position and the takeover was done on each house individually, by throwing grenades and using sub-machine guns. Many of the attackers as well as the inhabitants of the houses were hit. At daybreak, the force encountered fierce resistance around the Mukhtar's house at the top of the hill. Here there were additional casualties and the lack of ammunition made the rescue of the wounded difficult. At this stage, an appeal was made to the "Haganah" commander in Jerusalem, who provided Sten and rifle ammunition as well as covering fire to rescue the casualties. In the battle for Deir Yassin, 5 of our fighters fell, while the number of Arab casualties is disputed. According to the version accepted for years, about 250 Arabs were killed. This amazing number caused panic in the Arab population and indirectly influenced its flight from the Land of Israel during the 1948 battles. Recent studies cast doubt on the number of Arab casualties and the realistic assumption states that the number of Arab casualties was about 110. Beyond the debate over the number of casualties, everyone agrees that the battle of Deir Yassin was a key battle in the history of the War of Independence.<br><br><strong>5.5 Ramla</strong><br>In the middle of May, "Etzel" undertook to attack Ramla, at the request of the "Haganah", in order to draw enemy forces away from the Latrun front. "Etzel" mortar gunners bombarded the Arab city, attacked Arab Legion positions and occupied other sections of the city. The attacks began on 7 Iyar (May 16, 1948), and continued until 5th Sivan (June 11, 1948). The attacks failed in spite of fighters bravery and the assistance of the Givati Brigade, mainly because of a shortage of arms and ammunition. In the attacks and counter-attacks, dozens were wounded and 51 "Etzel" fighters were killed.`,
      `This area displays the capture of Arab villages by the Etzel during the War of Independence. One of these villages was Ras el Ein – known today as Rosh Ha'ayin.
According to the Partition Plan, Rosh Ha'ayin was slated to be part of the Arab state. The springs of the Yarkon River, located in this area, served as Jerusalem's most important source of water and there was genuine concern that the Arab forces under the command of Hassan Salameh would contaminate or block this precious water supply.
Therefore, at the end of May 1948, the Etzel and the Haganah decided to capture Rosh Ha'ayin and take control of the Yarkon river springs from the Arabs. After Hassan Salameh was killed in the battle and his men retreated, the Etzel forces captured the area.
A few days later, Salameh's men regrouped and attacked Rosh Ha'ayin. Too few to hold the area, the Etzel members had to retreat. Subsequently Rosh Ha'ayin and the Yarkon river springs were re-captured by the IDF's Alexandroni Brigade.
We're now standing in a special part of the museum which recounts the story of two Etzel heroines.
On May 4th, 1948, ten days before the Declaration of Independence, the village of Yehudiyya (modern day Yehud) was captured by Etzel forces.  The village was the local center of operations for gangs from the Lod district, headed by Hassan Salameh. Most of the Arab assailants who attacked Jewish convoys in this area were based in Yehudiyya.
Five weeks later, on June 11th, 1948, a large, well-armed unit belonging to the Arab Legion made a surprise attack against Etzel's forces in Yehudiyya, and the village fell.
As the retreating Etzel forces were about to depart, a voice came through on their receiver:  "Commander, I can hear Arab voices and I don't know what to do." It was the voice of Miriam Aharoni- the watcher who had remained on the bulwark of the local mosque and was now surrounded by Arab forces.
Commander Ruth Moritz, a new immigrant from Australia, grabbed a sub-machine gun and ran towards the mosque to rescue her subordinate.
Ruth managed to reach the mosque, climbed up the spiral staircase and found Miriam, frightened and helpless. The two immediately decided to fight to the last bullet.
When their ammunition ran out, preferring death to being taken captive by the Arabs, the women jumped from the bulwark.
Ruth, who was known by her code name "Devorah," had been engaged to Etzel fighter Avraham Canaani who was killed two weeks earlier in the battle of Jaffa. Instead of staying home to mourn him, she had returned to the front, where she fell heroically.
We have dedicated this corner of the museum to the memory of these two brave female fighters.
In front of you is a model of the Yehudiyya mosque and behind you is a map of the region with additional details.
You are also invited to view a video with testimonies of the battle.`,
      'inline'
    ),
    createExhibit(6, '6. Armament & Weapons Train',
      `<strong>6.1 Armament</strong><br>Facing the needs of open warfare, increased effort was devoted to arms production. Alongside continued operations to confiscate weapons from the British and based on the existing infrastructure from past times, many spare parts, sub-machine guns, hand grenades, 2" and 3" mortars were manufactured within a few months. The difficulties involved in obtaining explosives gave an added impetus to home manufacture. And, indeed, within a short time, a stock pile of explosives and a considerable quantity of mercury fulminate for detonators and percussion caps for bullets had been prepared. The work was done in factories of members and sympathizers as well as in Etzels Production Department.<br><br><strong>6.2 Training</strong><br>The transition from underground force to regular army created a change in the structure of Etzels units and forced it to adapt to the new conditions. Permanent bases were set up, to train veteran fighters and new enlistees: field training, battle manoeuvres and use of light weapons. Officers courses were held in the same bases. The bases served as take-off points for operating and were used to prepare for the integration of the "Etzel" fighters into the I.D.F.<br><br><strong>6.3 Confiscations and Aircraft Acquisition</strong><br>21 Kislev  – Dec 4, 1947: Confiscation of weapons from British soldiers in Tel Aviv streets.<br>5 Adar I  – Feb 15, 1948: Confiscation of supplies from Shekem warehouses (British) in Hadera.<br>8 Adar I  – Feb 18, 1948: Confiscation of weapons from British soldiers in Jerusalem streets.<br>12 Adar I  – Feb 22, 1948: Confiscation of military vehicles in Jerusalem.<br>4 Adar II  – Mar 15, 1948: Confiscation of weapons from a military camp in Beer Yaakov.<br>8 Adar II  – Mar 19, 1948: Confiscation of supplies from Shekem warehouses (British) in Haifa.<br>18 Adar II  – Mar 29, 1948: Confiscation of equipment and maps from government survey offices.<br>21-22 Adar II  – Apr 1-2, 1948: Confiscation of an armored car in Hadera and a half-track in Binyamina.<br>26 Adar II  – Apr 6, 1948: Confiscation of weapons from a military camp (Camp 80) in Pardes Hanna.<br>4 Nisan  – Apr 13, 1948: Confiscation of an armored car in Rishon LeZion.<br>16 Nisan  – Apr 15, 1948: Confiscation of weapons from two military wagons near Rehovot.<br>18 Nisan  – Apr 17, 1948: Confiscation of 20,000 3-inch mortar shells from a British train in Pardes Hanna.<br>21 Nisan  – Apr 30, 1948: Confiscation of a large cargo of flour.<br><br><strong>Aircraft Acquisition:</strong><br>Adar II  – March 1948: Purchase of two twin-engine "Oxford" planes in South Africa. Iyar – May 1948: Purchase of a "Piper" plane in France; two "Lodestar" planes and a "Hudson" plane in Canada. Before that, a "Chorley" plane for long-range flights was purchased in Canada.`,
      `The mortar in front of you is one of two 3-inch mortars which the Etzel succeeded in capturing from a British base close to Rosh Ha'ayin. Because the fighters did not have time to take the explosives as well, the mortars were nicknamed "yetomot"- "orphans".
A month before the British left Palestine, "Delek" (which means fuel), Etzel's intelligence branch, learnt that a train laden with weapons and ammunition was to travel from Haifa to Tulkarem , delivering its cargo to Arab forces.
Etzel decided to capture the train and confiscate the arms and ammunition. The plan was to ambush the train by laying explosives at a bend along the route.
The train set out after a three-day delay, due to the British concern that the weapons would be captured by one of the Jewish underground organizations. When it arrived at the bend in the tracks, the explosives were detonated as planned, the locomotive veered off the rails and the train came to a halt.
The Etzel quickly discovered that the number of British soldiers on the train was greater than anticipated and that the wagons were armored. Etzel's Chief Operations Officer, Amihai "Gidi" Paglin, stood in front of the wrecked train and shouted to the British troops: "Captain John! Captain John! Listen, Listen! You are surrounded on every side by hundreds of armed Etzel fighters. We aim to confiscate the weapons. Surrende, you have no choice, we will not harm you. If you resist, not a single one of you will remain alive. We will blow up the entire train. Come out with your hands up! This is my last warning!"
When the British troops surrendered and emerged from the train, they discovered, to their great surprise, that the railway line had not been booby-trapped and that they were "surrounded" by 27 weary Etzel fighters armed with unusable weapons.
Once the British surrendered, the Etzel fighters began to unload the weapons from the train onto waiting trucks. When they realized that time was running short and British reinforcements were on their way, "Gidi" demanded that the British commander and his troops help transfer the weapons.
Thus it was that Etzel fighters and British soldiers joined together to unload some 50 tons of weapons and ammunition, including 20,000 mortar shells which would later be put to use by the Etzel's "orphan" mortars.
With his imagination, creativity, courage and great resourcefulness, "Gidi" succeeded in transforming a failed operation into a great success.`,
      [{ label: 'The Weapons Train', file: 'audio/en-6.mp3' }]
    ),
    createExhibit(7, '7. The Campaign for Jaffa',
      `<strong>7.1 The Campaign for Jaffa</strong><br>On Monday, April 25, 1948, during the week of Passover Festivities, an "Etzel" force set out to conquer Jaffa, the largest Arab city in Palestine, population of about 80,000, and to remove the danger it represented to the city of Tel-Aviv and its residents.<br>The objective of the operation was to eliminate the threat of Jaffa becoming a naval and land base for the Arab armies.<br>After three days of hard fighting, on April 28 the large Manshiya quarter was taken, with its mosque, Hassan Bek, which had cast a shadow of fear over Tel-Aviv. The other parts of the city held fast with the help of 4,500 British soldiers, but the "Etzel" mortars, relentlessly bombarding the center of the town, caused the mass flight of the Arab population. The attack on Manshiya began before the drafting of the operative sections of the agreement with the "Haganah", which had become effective on April 24, according to which the "Etzel" would act in coordination and agreement with the "Haganah" high command. This was another reason, besides the British factor, that motivated "Etzel" to have the agreement signed and to hand Manshiya over to the "Haganah", which had commenced operation Hametz.<br><br><strong>7.2 Words of the Irgun Commander</strong><br>"We are going out to conquer Jaffa. We are going out to one of the decisive battles in the campaign for Israel's independence. Know who stands before you. Remember who you left behind you. Before you is a cruel enemy who rose to destroy us. Behind you are your parents, our brothers and our children. Strike the enemy! Aim well. Save ammunition. Do not know mercy in battle, just as the enemy does not know mercy towards the sons of our people. Have pity on women and children. He who raises his hands in surrender, has saved his soul. He is a prisoner in your hands, do not harm him. Gonader Gideon, experienced in battles, will lead you to the attack. Remember the Flag of the Status. One direction is before you: Forward!"<br><br><strong>7.3 Conquest Planning (Planning Division)</strong><br>The conquest of Jaffa was planned by the Planning Division in five stages:<br>A. To cross the no-mans-land and advance in two heads with a two unit force, in the direction of the Italian School, and along the continuation of Barnett Street, towards the sea. The advance had to be made inside houses, by breaking through walls, and inside yards, while covering exposed areas with sandbags.<br>B. An attempt by the left column to break through the Arab positions near the Italian school and those along El-Alam Street, the strategy being to sprint towards the positions and blow them up with explosives, or - when that was not possible by blowing up houses close to the positions and taking advantage of the ruins to cover further advances.<br>C. Throwing all reserve forces into places where quick breakthrough and advance would be successful, to reach the sea, while reinforcing the left flank of the advance with sandbags and stabilizing the line of positions along this flank.<br>D. Mopping up the cut-off area by patrols of infantry units assisted by armored vehicles.<br>E. Scanning the area along the new line of positions in Boustrous Street by mobile patrols in preparation for further attacks in the direction of the town center.<br><br><strong>7.4 Operation "Hametz"</strong><br>Operation Hametz (Clean-up Campaign), which commenced on April 27, 1948, had two objectives:<br>To surround and defeat Jaffa, and to open the road to Lydda airport.<br>The conquest of Jaffa was not included in the operation plans, because it was assumed that when the British left the city the population would be forced to surrender, as a result of the siege.<br>Three "Haganah" brigades participated in the campaign: Alexandroni, Givati and Kiryati.<br>The campaign commenced on the night of the 27-28 April, 1948, at the same time as the "Etzel" conquered Manshiya.<br>During the campaign the Tel Litvinsky military base (Tel Hashomer) and the Salame and Yazur villages, which served as principal bases for the Arab forces in the region, were taken, as well as Hiriya and Sakiya.<br>At the request of the "Haganah", an "Etzel" mortar unit assisted in the conquest of Salame.<br>However, the attack on Tel A-Rish failed and many "Haganah" men of the Givati Brigade were killed and wounded.<br>On April 30, after its residents had fled in masse, the Iraqi commander of Jaffa left the city with his men and their weapons.<br>On May 13, representatives of the Arabs of Jaffa signed a surrender agreement with the "Haganah" commander.<br>The British left Jaffa, upon termination of the Mandate, and "Haganah" and "Etzel" units victoriously entered the city.`,
      `We are now approaching the museum's main exhibit.
The area where the museum is located was formerly known as the "Manshiyya" neighborhood. It was a mixed neighborhood of Jews and Arabs. On the small map facing you, you can see that this was the northernmost neighborhood of Jaffa, close to Tel Aviv.
Following the rise in tensions during 1947, the last Jewish residents left the neighborhood and, at the beginning of the War of Independence, the last Arab residents left as well. Arab forces took over the neighborhood, erected concrete positions, and fired at the streets of Tel Aviv and its residents from the minaret of the Hassan Bek Mosque.  156 people were killed and over 1,000 were injured by the sniping.
The Etzel decided to capture the Manshiyya neighborhood and put an end to the sniping. The operation began on April 25th, 1948, during the Passover holiday.
Some 600 Etzel fighters attempted to take over the Arab positions using improvised bombs made from oil cans, explosive materials and slow-burning fuses.
After two days of fighting, Etzel commander-in-chief Menachem Begin ordered his men to withdraw because of the superiority of the enemy force. No important position had been taken and the route to the front remained blocked. However the organization's fighters refused. "Gidi" asked Begin for a 24-hour extension in order to plan a new operation. He noticed that the Manshiyya neighborhood consisted of houses that stood adjacent to each other and were made of soft limestone, just like the museum walls behind you.
Gidi suggested reducing his force to just 200 fighters who would hack through the houses with hammers, axes and chisels. Within a few hours, Etzel fighters captured the first reinforced position and, on the same day, reached the last Arab stronghold in the neighborhood, close to the location of the museum.  As a result of Etzel's operation, the Arab forces withdrew southward.
In light of what took place at Manshiyya, the British positioned warships off the coast of Tel Aviv and threatened to shell the city from the air, sea and land if Etzel did not return Manshiyya to the Arabs or to the British. Menachem Begin's response: "For four years, we fought against you and we never hurt your civilian population or even thought of doing so. But if you dare to hurt the Jewish civilian population of Tel Aviv, we shall respond and we will hurt your population. Your blood is not bluer than ours." The British opened fire against the Etzel forces in order to reconquer the neighborhood but were unsuccessful. They sought the assistance of Yisrael Rokach, Mayor of Tel Aviv and, together with Rokach, arrived at a compromise: the Manshiyya neighborhood would remain in Jewish hands and be put under the control of Haganah forces.`,
      [{ label: 'The Battle for Jaffa', file: 'audio/en-7.mp3' }]
    ),
    createExhibit(8, '8. "Etzel" in Jerusalem: Ramat Rachel, Malha & "Kedem"',
      `<strong>8.1 "Etzel" in Jerusalem</strong><br>During December 1947 - January 1948, the "Etzel" attacked the centers of Arab gangs and at the same time continued with its defense of the neighborhoods, in coordination with the "Haganah". On April 10 it took over Deir Yassin together with "Lehi".<br>Just before the British left Eretz Israel on the 14th of May 1948, an agreement was made between the "Haganah" and the "Etzel". The "Etzel" forces participated in operation "Kilshon", to occupy the areas vacated by the British, to halt the Arab Legion at Sheikh Gerach, to repulse the Egyptian army from Ramat Rachel, and to conquer Malha. They also took part in operation "Kedem" breaking into the Old city.<br>On September 22, 1948, the "Etzel" was disbanded in Jerusalem and its troops enlisted in the I.D.F. forces.<br><br><strong>8.2 Conquest of Malha Village</strong><br>14th-15th July, 1948.<br>An "Etzel" company set out to take the Arab village, MALHA, in cooperation with the "Haganah", with the aim of clearing the Arab forces out of the south-west area of Jerusalem.<br>MALHA was held by Egyptian forces and a small irregular force. The village conquered by the "Etzel" company.<br>The next day, the enemy counter-attacked with large forces supported by artillery. The "Etzel" fighters, who had not yet had time to dig in, suffered heavy losses. The Arabs abused the bodies of the dead and killed many wounded. The dead numbered 17.<br>"Etzel" reinforcements arriving from Katamon, repulsed the attackers and MALHA returned to Jewish hands.<br><br><strong>8.3 Operation "Kedem"</strong><br>An operation conducted on July 17, 1948, with the aim of breaking into the Old City from two directions "The New Gate" in the north and "Zion Gate" in the south. A battalion from the Etzioni Brigade of the IDF was tasked with attacking from the direction of Mount Zion; two Irgun companies were tasked with breaking through the northern section of the wall, in the area of "The New Gate". A Lehi company was tasked with penetrating through a breach in the wall between Jaffa Gate and "The New Gate". Heavy shelling by the Arab Legion prevented Etzioni and Lehi forces from carrying out the tasks assigned to them. Irgun soldiers breached "The New Gate" and found shelter inside a niche in the wall. They did not succeed in taking control of the vital post at the north-western corner of the wall. The time of the second truce was approaching and the Irgun commander suggested to the Jerusalem District Commander to remain in place even after the truce came into effect and try to stabilize a bridgehead inside the wall as a fait accompli. The proposal was not accepted, because the Jerusalem District Commander saw this as contrary to the Security Council resolution. At 05:45 fire ceased, and the breaching force left the Old City for its positions in Notre Dame.`,
      `Kibbutz Ramat Rachel was built on a small hill overlooking the Bethlehem-Jerusalem road. The hill is surrounded on all sides by Arab villages and neighborhoods and, to the west, lies the Mar Elias Monastery which served as the invading Egyptian army's position during the War of Independence.
On May 21st, 1948, Ramat Rachel came under heavy bombardment by the Arab forces.  The 80 kibbutz members who tried to defend the site were unable to withstand the attack and were forced to withdraw. The Arabs entered the kibbutz and began looting it. In the evening, reinforcements were sent to recapture the kibbutz. Two Haganah platoons stormed the kibbutz, chased the Arabs away and recaptured the site without a battle. Ramat Rachel was once again in Jewish hands.
On May 24th, 1948, Ramat Rachel was handed over to an Etzel unit under the command of Yehuda Lapidot – who was known as "Nimrod." According to an earlier agreement between Haganah and Etzel commanders, the site was to be manned by 6 platoons – 2 Haganah platoons, two Etzel platoons and two Lehi platoons. However, the Etzel unit was the only one to turn up. On the same day, Ramat Rachel was besieged by a formidable attack. 65 Etzel fighters armed with two machine guns and a crate of Molotov cocktails faced armored columns of the oncoming Egyptian army.
The kibbutz fell under heavy cannon and mortar fire. One of the shells hit the roof of the dining room and seriously wounded the soldiers on guard there. Ammunition began to run out, the number of wounded Etzel members grew, the paramedic himself was wounded and communication with other Jewish positions was lost. The Arab attack had been well planned and the Etzel's forces situation was dire. The kibbutz was completely surrounded and the dining room remained the last holdout. Due to the large number of wounded fighters, Etzel faced a serious shortage of fighters.
"We lay on the ground and, every time a shell exploded near us, there was a deafening noise and a hail of stones and dust fell on our heads. After each explosion, everyone raised their head to look around, for each one of us felt they were the only ones left alive and everything around was destroyed," recounts one of the fighters.
The Egyptian forces began to advance towards the dining room. Orders were to save ammunition and aim carefully. The Egyptian infantry which was advancing behind the armored columns launched the assault and only then did the Etzel's fighters fire back with every weapon at their disposal. The enemy suffered heavy losses and the assault was halted. As night fell, a reinforcement of Palmach fighters arrived. Apparently, the Haganah's headquarters announced that Ramat Rachel had fallen to the hands of the enemy and the fate of the defenders was unknown.
While the Etzel fighters were overjoyed at the sight of this hoped-for reinforcement, the Palmach fighters were even more surprised to discover that, despite rumors to the contrary, the kibbutz was still in Jewish hands.
One of the Palmach fighters recounted: "We reached Ramat Rachel. Who said that Etzel had retreated? It was a heroic battle fought by just a few Etzel units. For an entire day, isolated and cut off, they had to confront an onslaught by the Egyptian legionnaires. To be honest, we doubted the Etzel soldiers' fighting capability up until then, but their  unswerving stand at Ramat Rachel completely changed our view. We were amazed to see so many wounded men continue to fight to the last bullet."`,
      [{ label: 'Ramat Rachel', file: 'audio/en-8.mp3' }]
    ),
    createExhibit(9, '9. Mishmar HaYarden',
        `<strong>9.1 Mishmar HaYarden (Song)</strong><br>Lyrics: Naftali Herz Imber (Author of "Hatikvah") | Melody: Abraham Zvi Idelsohn. Onward Jordan, Onward flow! Your waves shall roar, Upon your banks wash and roll The scum of your land. Like the sound of thunder thunder a voice In the multitude of your waters The path of Zion pave for us We are behind you. Onward Jordan, Onward flow It shall be written for generations, That night and day We are on guard. Onward Jordan, Onward flow Your breakers shall roar, Break bonds, Break the yoke placed upon you. Bury them there in the banks of sand And thunder with your waves The Sons of Jacob shall hear the voice They shall come to your aid! Wave to wave, Deep to deep Shall then tell the events That night and day We are on guard.<br><br><strong>9.2 History: The Colony and the Underground</strong><br>The settlement Mishmar HaYarden was founded in 1890 on the grounds of the private farm Shoshanat HaYarden on the road between Rosh Pina and the center of the Golan Heights, near the Bnot Ya'akov Bridge.<br>The settlement suffered from security problems and economic instability, and developed slowly. Despite their efforts over the years, the settlers did not meet with economic success.<br>During the riots of 1936-1939, Mishmar HaYarden suffered greatly from attacks by Arab neighbors. Two farmers, a father and son, Haim and Menahem Grabovski, were murdered on their way to pumping water from the Jordan River.<br>In the late 1930s, members of the Beitar settlement platoon arrived to reinforce the settlement. National and regional "Etzel" commanders courses started to take place there. The settlement's unique topographic conditions enabled training with weapons, the production of arms, and to engage in fieldcraft.<br>In 1939, 42 "Etzel" officer course trainees were arrested by the British police while training with explosives. The 38 men were sentenced and imprisoned in Acre Prison, and the four women were sent to the Bethlehem Women's Prison.<br>At the beginning of 1947, members of the Wedgwood Organization settled Mishmar HaYarden. The Wedgwood Organization, named after Josiah Wedgwood, the British statesman and supporter of the Zionist cause, was founded in 1946 by about 70 Beitar and "Etzel" members, men and women, mostly British Army veterans or Holocaust survivors.<br><br><strong>9.3 Defense and Fall to the Syrians</strong><br>At the beginning of 1948, two separate defense groups were active in Mishmar HaYarden: seven farming families who were connected to the "Haganah" through the settlement's commander Natan Adler, and members of the Wedgwood group commanded by Shmuel Ben-Avraham, associated with "Etzel" headquarters.<br>With Syrian Army forces lined up along the border, the people of Mishmar HaYarden requested permanent reinforcement from the "Haganah". When the command of the Brigade refused, they decided to turn to "Etzel".<br>Indeed, at the beginning of April 1948, a reinforcement of 12 "Etzel" people was on its way to help Mishmar HaYarden which was under persistent attack from the Arabs. The group was stopped in a Palmach roadblock near Kibbutz Ginosar, and its members were told to get off the bus and submit their weapons. When they refused, they were taken by force to the kibbutz, beaten, stripped of their weapons, and deported back to Tel Aviv.<br>The "Haganah" people rationalized that this action was to ensure unity on the command level, a claim not accepted by "Etzel" headquarters. Several days later, the "Haganah" approved of the group's return to Mishmar HaYarden, where it became part of the settlement's defense force.<br>As of May 17, 1948, three days after the declaration of the establishment of the State of Israel, the Syrians started attacking Mishmar HaYarden and neighboring settlements with various types of weapons. The defenders of Mishmar HaYarden prepared for overall defense. At this stage, the defense force of the settlement included about 40 people: local settlers, Wedgwood group members, "Etzel" reinforcements, and reinforcements from neighboring settlements.<br>On June 5, a Syrian force of several infantry platoons backed by artillery attacked the settlement. Mishmar HaYarden defenders succeeded to halt the progress of enemy forces with machine gun force, and by the evening the enemy retreated.<br>The reinforcements of the Oded Brigade (former "Haganah" brigade that became part of the IDF) which came to the assistance of the settlement included two fighter squads of settlers from the settlement Yesod HaMa'alah, Kibbutz Ayelet HaShachar, and Kibbutz Hulata. The force endured artillery fire en route, suffering several losses, but eventually succeeded in joining the Mishmar HaYarden defenders.<br>At dawn of the next day, the Syrians renewed the attack with a force of more than 1,000 soldiers. The settlement was surrounded on three sides, cut off from Rosh Pina. The Syrians managed to occupy several posts. While the defenders' ammunition started to dwindle, and the situation seemed desperate, the persistence of the defenders to hold out until the last bullet together with IDF cannon fired towards the Syrians from Kibbutz Mahanaim led to breaking the attackers and to their retreat. Tens of Syrian casualties, wounded men, and many weapons were left behind.
<br><br><strong>9.4 The Last Battle and the Fall</strong><br>The second attack on Mishmar HaYarden started on the morning of June 10. A Syrian brigade force aided by armored vehicles, tanks, and planes breached the thin defense line held by 28 fighters.<br>The wounded and surviving fighters gathered in the cellar of one of the houses that also served as a clinic, and continued fighting from there. At noon, when ammunition ran out, and all hope was lost, the defenders surrendered to the attacking force.<br>One of the settlers, Eliezer Segal, went to surrender before the Syrians, but he was shot and killed on the spot.<br>Twenty-nine fighters, men and women, some of them wounded, were taken into captivity. Fifteen fighters fell in the Mishmar HaYarden battle.<br>The Syrian commanders could not comprehend how such a small number of fighters managed to endure their attacks during the four days of bloody battles.<br>The captives were released during the Syrian-Israeli prisoner of war exchange at the end of the War of Independence. The largest group was released from captivity on July 21, 1949.<br>Mishmar HaYarden has not been re-established after its complete destruction by the Syrians in the War of Independence. The settlement Bnei Tzfat was founded on its lands, later changing its name to Mishmar HaYarden - a moshav that belongs to the Herut-Beitar movement. Kibbutz Gadot is located nearby.<br>A monument is erected where the original Mishmar HaYarden houses stood in memory of the Mishmar HaYarden defenders who, with their bodies, blocked the way of invaders to the country, fell on their post, and sanctified with blood the honor of Israel on June 10, 1948. The names of the fifteen people who fell during the settlement's defense are engraved on the monument.`
    ),
    createExhibit(10, '10. Irgun in the Diaspora',
      `<strong>Irgun in the Diaspora</strong><br>Parallel to the campaign in Israel, the Irgun in the Diaspora continued with increasing activity. The Diaspora Headquarters, which was located in Paris (capital of France), commanded about 2,500 members who enlisted in 23 countries and were trained in commanders' courses and special camps. The Headquarters continued to organize attacks on British military targets and also developed extensive political activity, which reached its peak in May 1948 - with the de facto recognition by the French government of the Irgun command and the supply of weapons and military equipment free of charge in large quantities, which it allowed to be sent on "Altalena" to the Irgun in Eretz Israel. In the United States, the "Committee for the Liberation of the Nation" continued with political information among Congress members and the general public as well as raising funds for the Irgun. Donations from Irgun sympathizers in various countries were mostly transferred to the organization in Israel, and partly funded - purchase of weapons and explosives, mainly from Italy and France, the organization of the ship "Altalena" and its voyage, planes and maintenance of volunteers for the Irgun squadron as well as preparations for self-defense of Jews in North Africa. The Irgun headquarters in the Diaspora, established in January 1946, was disbanded in January 1949. Its members and subordinates immigrated or returned to Israel.`
         ),
    createExhibit(11, '11. Altalena',
      `<strong>11.1 Altalena</strong><br>On June 20, 1948, the "Etzel" vessel, "Altalena", berthed at Kfar Vitkin. There were 940 men and women on board and arms from France: guns, machine-guns, anti-tank and anti-aircraft weapons, half-tracks and ammunition.<br>Representatives of the interim government, who knew of the vessel's coming and agreed to its landing-place, rejected "Etzel"'s demand to set aside a certain percentage of the weapons for the Organization in Jerusalem and to use the rest to equip the "Etzel" battalions in the Israel Defence Forces.<br>In his speech on the Altalena affair, Begin said: "We could not agree to this. We repeatedly promised that we had no special intention in making this demand, and we suggested to them: You will supervise both our and your depots, and you will be in charge of this double guard. But they were adamant: 'No! These arms are for our disposal only...', and at the end of the discussions the Prime Minister announced that they would not help us to unload the arms. He added not one word more, but said only that they would not help us".<br>On the evening of the 21st June, fire was opened on the "Etzel" people at Kfar Vitkin and on the ship, which sailed to Tel Aviv. There it struck a reef off the Frishman beach, was shelled and burnt.<br>In the exchange of fire at Kfar Vitkin and Tel Aviv many Jewish fighters were killed - 16 "Etzel" members and three I.D.F soldiers.<br><br><strong>11.2 Song of "Altalena"</strong><br>We set out on the way to fight and suffer for you<br>We brought the spirit of revolt, a weapons ship for your liberation.<br>For long years in Europe we labored without pause<br>And the fruit of our labor Altalena we brought for you a state.<br>And if you ask us from where this weapon came to us<br>Then let Cain know that "Etzel" was and will be.<br>And how you received us, by God! We will never forget<br>Of soldier brothers we dreamed and were met with cannon fire.<br>And even though Altalena was sunk raise your heads soldiers<br>To the complete Land of Israel we shall remain forever loyal.<br>Rafael Kirsch z"l (Altalena immigrant – fell in battle in the Negev)<br><br><strong>11.3 Under Fire - Yiftach and the Flag</strong><br>Yiftach boarded "Altalena" abroad and functioned as a signalman. On 20.6.48 he was attached and posted to the Captain's room, where Menachem Begin was staying. With the shelling of "Altalena" off the Tel Aviv coast, Yiftach was among the last to jump from the burning ship. Upon reaching the vicinity of the shore, Yiftach looked back at the ship and his eyes caught the flag flying on the mast. Yiftach swam back to the ship, knowing the amount of ammunition in the belly of the ship going up in flames, which could explode at any moment. Yiftach lowered the flag, tied it to his body and swam under fire back to the shore. Yiftach enlisted in the IDF on 15.7.48 together with all Irgun men. Yiftach Stein was born in Czechia 21.7.1922 died 14.1.1992.`,
      `The Altalena- named after Ze'ev Jabotinsky's pen name- was a WW2 American landing craft which was purchased by the Etzel. On June 11th, 1948, the ship set sail for Israel from Port de Bouc in France, with 940 illegal immigrants on board, most of whom were Holocaust survivors, a group of Etzel fighters, military equipment, ammunition and a large amount of arms.  The departure had been delayed, and June 11th happened to be the date when the first UN-mediated truce during the War of Independence came into effect. The terms of the truce stipulated that the belligerent sides were not allowed to arm themselves or bring in reinforcements.
When Menachem Begin, the Etzel's chief commander, learnt of the ship's departure, he informed Yisrael Galili and Levi Eshkol, the representatives of the provisional government, of the ship's voyage, and asked for further instructions. Galili conveyed Prime Minister David Ben Gurion's instructions, according to which "the ship should arrive quickly" and the ship's captain, Eliahu Lankin, should unload its cargo on the Kfar Vitkin shore.  In the meantime, the two sides discussed the issue of transferring the arms to the IDF.  They agreed to allocate 20% of the cargo to Eztel's Jerusalem battalion.
According to the UN Resolution of November 29th, 1947, Jerusalem was to be under international rule, and the three underground movements – Haganah, Etzel and Lehi-continued to operate there independently.
At this point, two Etzel battalions – Battalion 57 in the Givati Brigade and Battalion 35 in the Alexandroni Brigade – had already been incorporated into the IDF. As per the agreement of June 1st, 1948 with the IDF, the Etzel's battalions were to enlist together with their equipment and weapons. Therefore, the Etzel's command proposed that the rest of the weapons on the Altalena be divided between Etzel battalions in the IDF. This proposal was rejected by representatives of the provisional government.
When the Altalena reached the Israeli shore on June 21st, 1948, the immigrants were quickly taken off board and dispersed. Etzel members began to unload the arms, assisted by members of the Palmach and local residents.
While Menachem Begin was holding a festive drill on the beach with the participation of the Etzel's senior command, he was handed a note with an ultimatum ordering him to hand over all the arms and ammunition within 10 minutes.  Begin asked for clarifications from the provisional government's representatives, but was refused. Then, suddenly, the ship and the Etzel group on the beach were fired upon. The Etzel command decided to stop unloading, raise anchor and sail towards the shores of Tel Aviv.
Off the shore of Tel Aviv, the IDF fired at the Altalena with light arms, grenades and artillery. Three shells were fired at the ship. The last shell caused the ship to go up in flames, and the Altalena began to sink. Begin ordered his men not to return fire and refused to come down from the deck, but his friends managed to get him off the ship by forming a human shield.
16 Etzel fighters and 3 IDF soldiers died in the "Altalena" incident.  Some 40 people were wounded. The Altalena immigrants heeded Begin's request and joined the ranks of the IDF`,
      [{ label: 'The Altalena', file: 'audio/en-11.mp3' }]
    ),
    createExhibit(12, '12. Story of a Shofar',
      `<strong>12.1 The Background: The British Ban</strong><br>Following the riots of 1929 (Tarpat) the British government appointed, with the consent of the League of Nations, a commission to determine the rights at the Western Wall. In the commission's conclusions it was forbidden for Jews to blow the shofar at the Western Wall plaza at the end of Yom Kippur. The Jewish Yishuv and the national institutions resented the order, but the Betar youth movement, and following it the Irgun, decided to violate this decree. Starting from the year 1930, determined young men arrived at the Wall and blew the shofar at the end of the "Neila" prayer, despite the presence of British soldiers and policemen.<br><br><strong>12.2 The Three Youths (1946)</strong><br>In the year 5707 (1946) the mission was imposed on three youths: Efraim Steinberg, Shmuel Bagayo and Moshe Karavani. The shofars were smuggled to the Western Wall plaza under the clothes of three female Betar members, knowing that the British soldiers would not conduct a search on their bodies. The three youths blew the shofar one after the other. Two succeeded in blending into the crowd and disappearing, but Moshe (Moishele) Karavani was caught. After a short interrogation he refused to give details about himself and the organization that sent him. It was decided to put him on trial. His lawyer, Eliyahu Meridor, succeeded in getting him acquitted by means of a legal loophole in the British statement of claim: It was not noted in the indictment that the youth who was caught is a Jew, and therefore there was no ground to accuse him of violating the order prohibiting Jews from blowing the shofar.<br><br><strong>12.3 Closing a Circle: The Shofar Returns</strong><br>Shmuel Bagayo told this story on a radio program. Some time later, a man from Scotland, who served in the Mandate Police in the Land of Israel in the year 1946, listened to the program. The former policeman made contact and told that he was the one who confiscated the shofar from one of the youths who were caught (in that period many shofars were confiscated). He asked to return the shofar to its owner. A mutual acquaintance connected the parties and the shofar was returned to the hands of Shmuel Bagayo. After his death his widow, Elisheva Bagayo, transferred the shofar for safekeeping to the "Etzel" Museum in 1948.`,
      `Following the 1929 Arab riots, an international committee determined that the Jews were legally forbidden to blow the shofar at Jerusalem's Western Wall.
The entire Yishuv reacted with fury, but was forced to accept the unjust decision. In response, the Beitar youth movement, and the Etzel later on, continuously attempted to violate the order. From 1930 onward, they succeeded in blowing the shofar at the conclusion of Yom Kippur (the Jewish Day of Atonement) while masses of worshippers were gathered at the Wall.
In 1946, the task was assigned to three young Beitar members: Ephraim Steinberg, Shmuel Bajayo and Moshe Karavani. The shofars were smuggled to the Wall by three female Beitar members who hid them under their dresses, knowing the British soldiers would not search their bodies. At the conclusion of the Yom Kippur service, Ephraim Steinberg, who was standing at the southern end of the Wall, was the first to blow the shofar. At the sound of the shofar, the British policemen rushed towards Steinberg but he managed to discard the instrument and vanish in the crowd. Shmuel Bajayo, who was standing in the central area, also succeeded in blowing the shofar for a short while before discarding it and fleeing.
Moshe Karavani, who was standing at the northern end of the Wall, was slow to flee and was subsequently caught. After a brief interrogation at the police station, during which Karavani refused to divulge details about himself and the organization which had assigned him the mission, he was sent forward for trial.
Karavani was acquitted thanks to his lawyer, Eliahu Meridor, who found a discrepancy in the British charge sheet: while the Mandatory regulations stipulated that only Jews were forbidden from blowing the shofar at the Western Wall, the charge sheet omitted to state that Karavani was Jewish and it therefore had no authority to prosecute him. Karavani was released, and his shofar was confiscated by the British.
Many years after the incident, an emissary from the state of Israel was sitting with a friend at a café in Scotland when, suddenly, an elderly Scotsman approached their table and asked to join them for a round of beer. This Scotsman went on to invite the men to his house because, apparently, he had a story to tell them. This was his story:
"After World War II, I agreed to serve in the British Police force in Palestine and was assigned to the Mahane Yehuda police station in Jerusalem.  At the approach of the Jewish holidays, we were given the task of preventing "criminals" from blowing the shofar and  infringing the orders of the Mandate authorities. Yom Kippur arrived and, together with a number of policemen dressed in civilian clothes, we took our positions at the Western Wall. We hid among the worshippers, waiting for the shofar to blow. When we heard the first blow, all the policemen, myself included, ran into the crowd to catch the offender but failed to do so. Then another blow was heard from the center and our attention was turned in that direction, but again without success.
There was much commotion as the elated worshippers sang defiantly at us. Then another blast of the shofar was heard, not far from the police sentry box. This blast lasted a long time and enabled us to arrest a small boy holding a shofar in his hand.
I looked at the swarthy little boy and was overcome with shame: the might of the great British Empire had been directed against this youth. In the end, the boy was allowed to go home but an inexplicable urge impelled me to hide the little shofar and take it with me. For many years, the shofar lay in my home. Now I appeal to you to take it, find its owner who brought great shame on the British Empire and return it to him."
A mutual acquaintance who heard the story from both Shmuel Bajayo and the emissary upon his return from Scotland brought the two sides together, and thus it was that the shofar was returned to one of the blowers, Shmuel Bajayo. After his death, his widow donated the shofar to our museum. `,
      [{ label: 'Story of a Shofar', file: 'audio/en-12.mp3' }]
    ),
    createExhibit(13, '13. Integration of the Irgun into the IDF',
      `With the declaration of the State on 5 Iyar  (May 14, 1948), the Irgun commander, Menachem Begin, announced that the organization would emerge from the underground within the boundaries of the Jewish State. On June 1, 1948, an agreement was signed detailing enlistment requirements in accordance with General HQ Circular No. 19/10/319: "It is agreed that "Etzel" members will enlist individually and will operate during the transition period in battalions within our brigades and a temporary Headquarters of former "Etzel" officers will operate under the General HQ until the enlistment of all members of "Etzel" into the I.D.F. has been completed." Two battalions were established: "Battalion 35" in the Alexandroni Brigade and "Battalion 57" in the Givati Brigade. A further battalion which was being set up was disbanded following the "Altalena" affair and all "Etzel" soldiers enlisted the I.D.F. - some in company frameworks and the majority as individuals. The number of "Etzel" fighters who fell in the battles of Israel's War of Independence totalled 321. (194 fell among the ranks of the I.D.F., and 127 while in the "Etzel" framework.)`
    )
